2026/05/04 08:13:58 INFO dspy.teleprompt.simba_utils: Advice for nl2pln.predict: If a sentence says an entity is adjective-as one reference object but opposite-adjective-as another reference object, encode the comparison explicitly with `As` predicates and stable lowercase constants, e.g. for “The Knife is long and sharp as a kitchen knife but short and blunt as a sashimi knife,” prefer `(: s0 (Knife knife) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, `(: s1 (LongAs knife kitchen_knife) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, `(: s2 (SharpAs knife kitchen_knife) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, `(: s3 (ShortAs knife sashimi_knife) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, `(: s4 (BluntAs knife sashimi_knife) (STV 1.0 1.0))`; avoid plain forms like `(Long Knife kitchen_knife)` because later questions need the comparative relation. When the contrast implies an ordering between the reference objects, also add or enable a direct relation such as `(ShortAs kitchen_knife sashimi_knife)` so a question about the reference classes can be answered. For questions, reuse the exact comparative predicates from context: “Is the knife long as compared to a different knife?” should query `(: $prf (LongAs knife kitchen_knife) $tv)` if `kitchen_knife` is the known comparator, or `(: $prf (LongAs knife $otherKnife) $tv)` only when the question genuinely asks to retrieve the comparator. Do not use `LikelierThan` for ordinary size/length comparisons; it compares truth strengths of hypotheses and can time out. For “Are A shorter than B?” query a single direct relation like `(: $prf (ShortAs kitchen_knife sashimi_knife) $tv)`, not an `And` of unrelated facts such as `(Short ...)` and `(Long ...)` unless those exact facts exist and a conjunction is explicitly requested.
