2026/05/04 09:00:11 INFO dspy.teleprompt.simba_utils: Advice for nl2pln.predict: If the input sentence says that several named people are doing something "with each other" or "to each other" with homework help, do not encode it as one group/n-ary predicate such as `(: s1 (HelpingEachOtherWithHomework mark bob anna) (STV 1.0 1.0))`. Instead, create explicit binary facts for every ordered pair of distinct people using a simple predicate whose first argument is the helper/provider and second argument is the recipient, e.g. for `Mark, Bob and Anna are helping each other with homework`, output `(: s0 (HelpingWithHomework mark bob) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, `(: s1 (HelpingWithHomework mark anna) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, `(: s2 (HelpingWithHomework bob mark) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, `(: s3 (HelpingWithHomework bob anna) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, `(: s4 (HelpingWithHomework anna mark) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, and `(: s5 (HelpingWithHomework anna bob) (STV 1.0 1.0))`. If later asked `Does Mark help Anna with homework?`, query the exact binary fact already represented: `(: $prf (HelpingWithHomework mark anna) $tv)`, with no new statements. If asked `Who is providing help with homework?`, query the provider/helper position with the recipient left as a variable: `(: $prf (HelpingWithHomework $who $x) $tv)`. Use lowercase constants for names, keep proof IDs non-variable in statements, and avoid broad or invented n-ary queries like `(HelpingEachOtherWithHomework $who $x $y)` when a binary relation is the natural meaning.
